
Tantrasāra — Chapter 1
Abhinavagupta, circa 1000 CE · Slang Edition

The Essence of Tantra: or, why you’re literally Śiva and somehow haven’t clocked it yet.

Alright, listen up.

I’ve written this absolutely massive book called the Tantrāloka. Proper monster of a text. Not everyone’s got the time or mental bandwidth to get through it.

So here’s the Tantrasāra. Same essential teaching, less messing about.

First, massive respect to the divine Mother and Father, Śakti and Śiva. And to my teacher Śambhunātha, whose wisdom opened my heart like a lotus.

Right. Let’s get into it.

Here’s the situation.

You’re bound because you’re ignorant.

And you’re liberated through knowledge.

Simple as that.

But before you go thinking I’m talking about reading a few books and becoming some enlightened intellectual, absolutely not.

There are actually two kinds of ignorance.

The first is bauddha-ajñāna.

That’s when your understanding is all over the place. You don’t know what’s real, or worse, you’re completely convinced of something that’s wrong.

You genuinely believe you’re just this separate little individual knocking about in a universe full of other separate things.

The second is pauruṣa-ajñāna.

Now this one’s deeper.

This isn’t just having the wrong ideas. It’s the actual condition of experiencing yourself as a limited being.

Consciousness itself has contracted.

Saṃkoca.

The infinite has somehow started experiencing itself as finite.

Absolutely mental, when you think about it.

And this contracted condition is the root of saṃsāra.

Now, initiation — dīkṣā — can remove that deeper ignorance.

But here’s the thing.

You can’t just abandon understanding and expect everything to sort itself out.

You need proper discernment. You need to understand what you’re doing, what you’re seeking, and what needs to be abandoned.

And if you cultivate that understanding correctly, something remarkable happens.

Your conceptual knowledge gradually takes you beyond conceptual knowledge.

You use thought to get beyond thought.

Not because thinking is evil, but because correct thinking can dismantle the very misconceptions keeping consciousness contracted.

The destination isn’t a better concept of Śiva. It’s the recognition of your own nature as Śiva.

And that’s what we’re doing here.

Now, you might be wondering what exactly you’re supposed to recognise.

Good question.

Let’s talk about reality.

Look around you.

Everything you see, hear, feel, remember, imagine — absolutely everything that appears — appears through prakāśa.

The light of consciousness.

And no, mate, I don’t mean sunlight.

I mean the very fact that anything can manifest, can be known, can appear at all.

Try finding something outside the possibility of awareness.

Go on.

I’ll wait.

You can’t.

Even space and time, the things you imagine containing everything else, are themselves manifested in awareness.

So consciousness isn’t some little thing sitting inside your head, looking out at a universe.

It’s the very luminosity through which the universe appears.

And here’s where it gets interesting.

Consciousness doesn’t need another consciousness to illuminate it.

Because then you’d need another one to illuminate that one.

And another.

And another.

Congratulations, you’ve invented an infinite regress.

No thanks.

Consciousness is self-luminous.

It doesn’t borrow its light from something else.

It is svatantra — independent, sovereign, free.

And that freedom?

Svātantrya.

That’s the whole game.

Śiva is not some bloke sitting above the clouds deciding what happens next.

Śiva is this absolutely unrestricted, self-aware luminosity.

And because he’s free, he can manifest in every conceivable form without ceasing to be himself.

Now, that freedom expresses itself through five great powers.

You’ve got cit-śakti, the power of consciousness itself.

Ānanda-śakti, the bliss of being utterly free and complete.

Icchā-śakti, the power of will.

Jñāna-śakti, the power of knowing.

And kriyā-śakti, the power of action.

Don’t imagine five different substances.

It’s the same Śiva, considered through different aspects of his own freedom.

And now comes the really ridiculous part.

This utterly unlimited consciousness, resting in its own bliss, can freely manifest itself as limited.

The infinite appears as the tiny individual.

Aṇu.

That’s you.

Not because something outside Śiva has trapped him.

There’s nothing outside Śiva capable of doing that.

It’s his own freedom.

The very power through which consciousness manifests the entire universe also makes possible the appearance of limitation.

So what happens when that limitation is lifted?

Śiva recognises himself.

The same consciousness that appeared contracted shines again in its unrestricted nature.

And because beings experience this recognition differently, there are different approaches.

Some don’t require a method at all.

That’s anupāya.

Others recognise through the power of will, through pure awareness and immediate orientation.

That’s śāmbhavopāya.

Others work through refined understanding and contemplation.

That’s śāktopāya.

And others work through embodied practices, breath, concentration, and activity.

That’s āṇavopāya.

Four possibilities.

One reality.

And I’m going to explain each of them.

But understand this before we go any further.

You are not trying to manufacture Śiva.

You are not trying to construct some new divine identity.

The reality you’re seeking is already the nature of the very consciousness by which you’re seeking it.

The apparent limitation and its removal both occur through Śiva’s own freedom.

The entire spiritual journey is Śiva appearing to forget himself, and Śiva recognising himself again.

Right.

On to the methods.

This is a creative, slang-language retelling of Chapter 1, not Abhinavagupta’s exact words. It follows the Sanskrit text’s treatment of ignorance, liberating knowledge, prakāśa, svātantrya, the five śaktis and the four upāyas. gretil.uni-goettingen.de +2

